            <ArticleTitle>Effect of L-arginine administration on myocardial thallium-201 perfusion during exercise in patients with angina pectoris and normal coronary angiograms.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>97-102</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We tested the hypothesis that an exogenous supplement of L-arginine could alleviate coronary perfusion abnormality during exercise in patients with angina pectoris and normal coronary arteries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twelve patients underwent exercise thallium-201 scintigraphy without medication (control) and after intravenous administration of L-arginine. Exercise time was prolonged in the L-arginine study compared with the control (482 s vs 540 s, P &lt; .05). TI-201 extent score was improved in the L-arginine study (0.33 vs 0.26, P &lt; .05), and the severity score was also improved (23.7 vs 16.9, P &lt; .05). In 7 of the 12 patients whose TI-201 redistribution disappeared in the L-arginine study, the percent increase in serum L-citrulline concentration during exercise was larger than that of the remaining 5 patients (18% vs 0.9%, P &lt; .01). The percent reduction in epicardial coronary diameter in response to acetylcholine was also greater in the former group (28.3% vs 11.1%, P &lt; .05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Exogenous L-arginine improved myocardial perfusion during exercise in a subset of patients with angina pectoris and normal coronary arteries, probably by increasing production of nitric oxide.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
